   112	## Cutoff-driven historical learning (owner-mandated)
   113	
   114	### Scope and chronology
   115	
   116	- Every decision-facing historical learning, calibration, tuning, selection, ranking, gate, or optimization function must receive an explicit cutoff, either as a standalone cutoff object or embedded in an explicitly passed decision object (for example, `portfolio$cutoff`). An embedded cutoff satisfies this requirement; do not require a redundant standalone cutoff argument.
   117	- Validate the embedded cutoff and its binding to the object's input snapshots and derived artifacts. Missing, malformed, or inconsistent cutoff context must fail closed; never infer it from the current clock, hidden globals, or mutable session state. Changing the cutoff requires rebuilding the affected decision artifacts, not merely editing their cutoff field. All chronology, receipt, and cache requirements below apply equally to both forms.
   118	- This includes preprocessing, thresholds, support depth, hyperparameters, seeds, expert admission, routing, allocation, bankroll gates, league/team effects, regimes, embeddings, priors, shrinkage, imputation, normalization, and availability gates.
